Each curve traces the trajectory of True Reward (<math id="A3.F7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>) vs. Proxy Reward (<math id="A3.F7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}</annotation></semantics></math>) as the sample size <math id="A3.F7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> increases from <math id="A3.F7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A3.F7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>10</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>, with marker size proportional to <math id="A3.F7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. The smallest marker represents <math id="A3.F7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1</annotation></semantics></math> (standard sampling), while the largest represents <math id="A3.F7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1024</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Additional Results on Selected Configurations in Figures <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Deciding the Prompt-Dependent 𝛼(𝑥) ‣ 4 BoT: A Tail-Adaptive Alignment ‣ Best-of-Tails: Bridging Optimism and Pessimismin Inference-Time Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present the trajectory of true reward <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and proxy reward <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> versus the sample size <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> increases (top row), alongside the trade-off (bottom row) between proxy reward and policy distortion (KL, <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and Tsallis divergences) on GSM8K (Figure <a href="#A3.F8" title="Figure A.8 ‣ C.2 Additional Results on Selected Configurations in Figures ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Ablation Studies ‣ Best-of-Tails: Bridging Optimism and Pessimismin Inference-Time Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.8</span></a>), MMLU (Figure <a href="#A3.F9" title="Figure A.9 ‣ C.2 Additional Results on Selected Configurations in Figures ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Ablation Studies ‣ Best-of-Tails: Bridging Optimism and Pessimismin Inference-Time Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.9</span></a>), and MATH (Figure <a href="#A3.F10" title="Figure A.10 ‣ C.2 Additional Results on Selected Configurations in Figures ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Ablation Studies ‣ Best-of-Tails: Bridging Optimism and Pessimismin Inference-Time Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.10</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While <span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoN</span> maximizes the raw proxy reward, it suffers from reward hacking at large <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to lower true utility (accuracy).
In contrast, <span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoT</span> achieves the highest true reward among all strategies, surpassing both the conservative <span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ITP</span> and the optimistic baselines.
The distortion analysis further validates the efficiency of <span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoT</span>: it attains a higher proxy reward than <span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ITP</span> for the same level of distortion, yet maintains significantly tighter control over divergence than <span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sBoN</span> or <span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoN</span> as <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> scales, effectively balancing exploration with robustness.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="A3.F8.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.F8.2.1" class="ltx_text"><img src="2603.06797v1/app-figures/gsm8k-addition.png" id="A3.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/151;" width="381" height="151" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure A.8: </span><span id="A3.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The trajectory of true reward <math id="A3.F8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and proxy reward <math id="A3.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> versus the sample size <math id="A3.F8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> increases (top row), alongside the trade-off (bottom row) between proxy reward and policy distortion (KL, <math id="A3.F8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and Tsallis divergences) on GSM8K.
We compare optimistic (<span id="A3.F8.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sBoN</span>, <span id="A3.F8.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoN</span>), pessimistic (<span id="A3.F8.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ITP</span>), and adaptive (<span id="A3.F8.4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoT</span>) strategies using select configurations from Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Deciding the Prompt-Dependent 𝛼(𝑥) ‣ 4 BoT: A Tail-Adaptive Alignment ‣ Best-of-Tails: Bridging Optimism and Pessimismin Inference-Time Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="A3.F9.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.F9.2.1" class="ltx_text"><img src="2603.06797v1/app-figures/mmlu-addition.png" id="A3.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/149;" width="381" height="149" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure A.9: </span><span id="A3.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The trajectory of true reward <math id="A3.F9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and proxy reward <math id="A3.F9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> versus the sample size <math id="A3.F9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> increases (top row), alongside the trade-off (bottom row) between proxy reward and policy distortion (KL, <math id="A3.F9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and Tsallis divergences) on MMLU.
We compare optimistic (<span id="A3.F9.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sBoN</span>, <span id="A3.F9.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoN</span>), pessimistic (<span id="A3.F9.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ITP</span>), and adaptive (<span id="A3.F9.4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoT</span>) strategies using select configurations from Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Deciding the Prompt-Dependent 𝛼(𝑥) ‣ 4 BoT: A Tail-Adaptive Alignment ‣ Best-of-Tails: Bridging Optimism and Pessimismin Inference-Time Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<figure id="A3.F10" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="A3.F10.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.F10.2.1" class="ltx_text"><img src="2603.06797v1/app-figures/math-addition.png" id="A3.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/149;" width="381" height="149" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure A.10: </span><span id="A3.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The trajectory of true reward <math id="A3.F10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and proxy reward <math id="A3.F10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> versus the sample size <math id="A3.F10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> increases (top row), alongside the trade-off (bottom row) between proxy reward and policy distortion (KL, <math id="A3.F10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and Tsallis divergences) on MATH.
We compare optimistic (<span id="A3.F10.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sBoN</span>, <span id="A3.F10.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoN</span>), pessimistic (<span id="A3.F10.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ITP</span>), and adaptive (<span id="A3.F10.4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoT</span>) strategies using select configurations from Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Deciding the Prompt-Dependent 𝛼(𝑥) ‣ 4 BoT: A Tail-Adaptive Alignment ‣ Best-of-Tails: Bridging Optimism and Pessimismin Inference-Time Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Ablation Studies</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct ablation studies to analyze the sensitivity of the steering temperature <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and the tail pivot <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on the GSM8K dataset, utilizing <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemma-2-2B</span> as the reference policy and <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ARMO-RM</span> as the reward model.
For all experiments in this section, we fix the sample size at <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1024</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Steering Temperature <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> (Figure <a href="#A3.F11" title="Figure A.11 ‣ Tail Pivot 𝜅_0 (Figure , Right). ‣ C.3 Ablation Studies ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Ablation Studies ‣ Best-of-Tails: Bridging Optimism and Pessimismin Inference-Time Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.11</span></a>, Left).</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first fix <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to the median of <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\kappa}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\kappa}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> across prompts and sweep <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\{0.001,\dots,1.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.001</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\{0.001,\dots,1.0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the high-temperature regime (large <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>), the re-weighting term <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(r/\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(r/\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> flattens, causing the candidate distribution to approach uniformity; consequently, the behaviors of <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sBoN</span>, <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ITP</span>, and <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoT</span> converge.
As <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> decreases, the strategies diverge in how they prioritize high-reward responses.
<span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ITP</span> exhibits stable performance due to its linear constraint, whereas <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sBoN</span> becomes increasingly aggressive, converging toward the hard-maximization behavior of standard <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoN</span> (i.e., placing all weight on the single highest-reward candidate).
Crucially, while <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoT</span> does not always maximize the raw proxy reward—avoiding the over-optimization trap—its adaptive <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(x)</annotation></semantics></math> allows it to achieve higher <em id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">true</em> reward (accuracy) by mitigating tail risks that optimistic baselines ignore.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tail Pivot <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (Figure <a href="#A3.F11" title="Figure A.11 ‣ Tail Pivot 𝜅_0 (Figure , Right). ‣ C.3 Ablation Studies ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Ablation Studies ‣ Best-of-Tails: Bridging Optimism and Pessimismin Inference-Time Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.11</span></a>, Right).</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we fix <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0.01</annotation></semantics></math> and sweep <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}\in\{0.001,\dots,5.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0.001</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}\in\{0.001,\dots,5.0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sBoN</span> and <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ITP</span> are independent of <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, they appear as constant baselines.
The parameter <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> governs the transition point of our adaptive strategy:</p>
<ul id="A3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optimistic Limit (<math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>):</span>
As <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the term <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\hat{\kappa}(x)}{\hat{\kappa}(x)+\kappa_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\hat{\kappa}(x)}{\hat{\kappa}(x)+\kappa_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, driving <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(x)=1+\frac{\hat{\kappa}(x)}{\hat{\kappa}(x)+\kappa_{0}}\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(x)=1+\frac{\hat{\kappa}(x)}{\hat{\kappa}(x)+\kappa_{0}}\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>. In this regime, <span id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoT</span> mimics the optimistic behavior of <span id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sBoN</span>, as reflected in the proxy reward curves.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pessimistic Limit (<math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>):</span>
Conversely, as <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases below the median of <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\kappa}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\kappa}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the ratio approaches 1, driving <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(x)=1+\frac{\hat{\kappa}(x)}{\hat{\kappa}(x)+\kappa_{0}}\to 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(x)=1+\frac{\hat{\kappa}(x)}{\hat{\kappa}(x)+\kappa_{0}}\to 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <span id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoT</span> shifts toward the conservative, pessimistic profile of <span id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ITP</span>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The results demonstrate that by selecting an intermediate <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (calibrated to the median tail index), <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoT</span> successfully interpolates between these regimes, outperforming static baselines by adapting to the specific risk profile of each prompt.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F11" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="A3.F11.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.F11.2.1" class="ltx_text"><img src="2603.06797v1/app-figures/gsm8k-gemma-armo-ablation.png" id="A3.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/120;" width="476" height="120" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure A.11: </span><span id="A3.F11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation analysis of steering temperature <math id="A3.F11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="A3.F11.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">left two figures</span>) and tail pivot <math id="A3.F11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="A3.F11.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">right two figures</span>). We compare optimistic (<span id="A3.F11.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sBoN</span>, <span id="A3.F11.4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoN</span>), pessimistic (<span id="A3.F11.4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ITP</span>), and adaptive (<span id="A3.F11.4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BoT</span>) strategies. The black dashed line marks the median of the estimated tail indices <math id="A3.F11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\kappa}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\kappa}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> across all prompts.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
